{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795680974,"nonce":"Voc7Zp+BXL4ZO3ziEuf7B0pHcLg=","title":"SENT 3-D phase-field test pairs the two slab faces by index, and the tet mesh has 491 vs 492 nodes on them","message":"`tests/test_fem_phase_field_sent.py::test_sent_3d_reproduces_the_plane_strain_answer` fails on `main` before it reaches its physics checks. It compares the damage on the slab's two faces (z = 0 and z = thick) node by node, after sorting each face's nodes by (x, y). That assumes both faces carry the same nodes, as an extruded mesh would. The unstructured tet mesh of the notched box has 491 nodes on one face and 492 on the other, so the subtraction raises.\n\n**Reproduce**\n\n```\npytest -q \"tests/test_fem_phase_field_sent.py::test_sent_3d_reproduces_the_plane_strain_answer\"\n```\n\n`ValueError: operands could not be broadcast together with shapes (491,) (492,)` at the `face_gap` line. Seen on GPU on `main` (after #345), and the same with the phase-field `domain.unknown()` fixes on top, so those fixes do not cause it. The test is marked `slow`, and CI runs `-m 'not slow'`, so CI never reaches it; that is why it went unnoticed.\n\n**Expected**\n\nThe physics checks run: the 3-D damage at the end of the load path is through-thickness uniform to 5 % of its peak, and the peak matches the 2-D plane-strain run of the same load path to 15 %. The face comparison should not depend on the mesher placing identical nodes on both faces.\n\n**Where**\n\nThe test only: `test_sent_3d_reproduces_the_plane_strain_answer`, the `lo` / `hi` face masks and the two `lexsort` orders. The geometry is `jno.shape.box(...) - jno.shape.box(...)` at `size=0.06`, meshed as unstructured tets. No library code is involved in the failure.\n\n**Workaround**\n\nNone needed in the library. The 2-D test (`test_sent_2d_initiates_localizes_and_softens`) passes and covers the march, the bounds and the staggered solve.\n\n**Direction**\n\nCompare the faces by position, not by index: interpolate one face's damage at the other face's (x, y) points (nearest neighbour, or linear over the face triangulation), or sample both on a common (x, y) grid, then take the largest gap over the peak. Alternatively mesh the slab extruded in z, so the faces match by construction. After the change, check that the 5 % tolerance still separates a uniform field from one that varies through the thickness (for example, by perturbing one face's values in the test).","files":null}]}